\title[Poisson summation characterises subgroups]{Poisson Summation Characterises Subgroups:\\ A Complete Converse, with Exact Defect and a Rigidity Gap}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $G$ be a finite abelian group with character group $\widehat{G}$. For a subgroup $H \le G$ with annihilator $H^{\perp} = \{\psi \in \widehat{G} : \psi|_H \equiv 1\}$, the finite Poisson summation formula states that $|G| \sum_{x \in H} f(x) = |H| \sum_{\psi \in H^{\perp}} \hat{f}(\psi)$ for every $f : G \to \mathbb{C}$. The shape of this identity makes sense for an arbitrary subset $S \subseteq G$, since the annihilator $S^{\perp}$ is defined for any $S$; we call $S$ a \emph{Poisson set} when the corresponding identity holds for all $f$. We prove the complete converse: $S$ is a Poisson set if and only if $S = \emptyset$ or $S$ is a subgroup, the empty set being a genuine and unique exceptional solution. We establish four strengthenings: one-test-function rigidity (a single Dirac delta suffices), an exact defect formula identifying the failure of the identity with the discrepancy between averaging over $S$ and over the generated subgroup $\gen{S}$, a gap theorem showing the defect of a nonempty non-subgroup is at least $|\gen{S}| - |S| \ge 1$, and rigidity of the normalising constant. Consequences include an affine version characterising cosets, the identification of Poisson sets with extremals of the Donoho--Stark uncertainty principle, an order isomorphism between the Poisson spectrum and the subgroup lattice, the count $d(n)$ of exact Poisson formulas on $\Zn{n}$, and the separation of $\Zn{4}$ from the Klein four-group. We apply the results to quadratic residues: the squares modulo $n$ are Poisson sets only for $n \in \{1,2\}$, and modulo $8$ they fail with a defect of exactly $5$.
\end{abstract}

